{
  "assessment": "The change introduces four new consumer modules for billing, retry logic, SLA evaluation, and job summarization. None of these modules account for the `CANCELLED` status added in 4.2, leaving cancelled jobs incorrectly treated as chargeable, retriable, and currently-in-flight across all new decision paths. This propagates the exact operational flaw into the freshly added consumers rather than fixing it.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "actual": "`now` is returned instead of `job.ended_at`, causing in-flight cancelled jobs to be compared against the current wall-clock time and incorrectly reported as SLA breaches.",
      "at": "jobs/sla.py",
      "call": "sla.finished_at(cancelled_job, now=1000) where job.ended_at=52",
      "defect": "`finished_at` falls back to `now` for any status not explicitly in `(DONE, FAILED)`.",
      "expected": "`job.ended_at` (e.g., 52), so the SLA deadline is correctly measured against when work actually stopped."
    },
    {
      "actual": "`True`, because `status != st.FAILED` and `cpu_seconds > 0`.",
      "at": "jobs/charges.py",
      "call": "charges.is_chargeable(cancelled_job_with_cpu)",
      "defect": "`is_chargeable` only excludes the `FAILED` status, leaving cancelled jobs to be billed for compute time that was halted by operation.",
      "expected": "`False`, as cancelled work yields no usable result and should not trigger billing or credit requests."
    },
    {
      "actual": "`True`, passing all checks since only `DONE` is explicitly excluded from retry candidates.",
      "at": "jobs/retry.py",
      "call": "retry.should_retry(cancelled_job)",
      "defect": "`should_retry` does not filter out `CANCELLED` jobs, allowing the scheduler to incorrectly mark halted work for automatic requeuing.",
      "expected": "`False`, as a cancelled job is a terminal state and must never be requeued."
    }
  ]
}
